  <h2>What's the difference between certifying your QMS and buying ISO 9001 software?</h2>
  <p>
    A certification body audits your organisation. If you meet the standard, it issues an ISO 9001
    certificate, a judgement about your management system made by an accredited third party.
    Software supports your management system by controlling documents, tracking approvals and keeping an
    audit trail. It is never itself "ISO 9001 certified", because certification applies to organisations.
  </p>
  <p>
    In South Africa, look for a certification body that is <strong>SANAS-accredited</strong>. SANAS, the
    South African National Accreditation System, accredits certification bodies for quality management
    systems and other schemes, so a SANAS-accredited certificate carries a documented chain of trust from
    SANAS to the certification body to your organisation. A consultant can help you prepare for that audit.
    Software like Obel MS gives your certification body the document control and audit trail evidence it
    will ask for, built around ISO 9001 clause 7.5. The certificate itself always comes from the
    accredited body.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
