        <tr><td>SANAS-accredited certification body</td><td>Audits your QMS and issues your ISO 9001 certificate</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ISO 9001 consultant</td><td>Helps you build and prepare your QMS for that audit</td></tr>
        <tr><td>QMS / document control software</td><td>Runs the document control, approvals and audit trail your QMS relies on day to day</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Many of the South African search results for "ISO 9001 software" are certification
    consultancies advertising audit and certification services. They solve a different problem from
    software. A consultancy helps you get certified. Software runs your document control day to day,
    before and after the audit. Ask early which one you are talking to.
  </p>

  <h2>What should a South African SME check about data residency and POPIA?</h2>
  <p>
    POPIA does not flatly require your data to stay inside South Africa, but it does put conditions on
    moving personal information to a third party abroad, and it makes you accountable either way. Before
    choosing ISO 9001 software, ask where the servers are and who is legally responsible for what.
  </p>
  <p>
    Under POPIA, the <strong>responsible party</strong> is whoever decides why personal information is
    collected and how it is processed. For your supplier records and quality documents, that is your own
    organisation. An <strong>operator</strong> processes that information on your behalf under contract,
    without setting its own purpose for it. That is the role a hosted QMS vendor plays for the records you
    put into its system. Ask any vendor to state, in writing, which role it plays.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Cross-border transfer</strong> is the specific POPIA question that matters most when your
    vendor's servers are outside South Africa. Section 72 restricts transferring personal information about
    a data subject to a third party in a foreign country unless one of several conditions is met, most
    commonly that the recipient is subject to a law, binding rules or an agreement providing an adequate
    level of protection similar to POPIA's own, or that the data subject has consented to the transfer.
    Hosting with a South African provider sidesteps that question entirely for data that never leaves the
    country.
  </p>
  <p>
    In practice, ask a vendor two direct questions: which country is your data physically stored in, and
    who else, such as subcontractors, subprocessors or a parent company abroad, might ever touch it? A
    vendor that can't answer both without checking should be asked again, well before you sign.
  </p>

  <h2>What else should you check before choosing ISO 9001 software in South Africa?</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond POPIA, a handful of practical checks separate a vendor built for South African SMEs from one that
    happens to sell here. Work through the table below with any vendor you're evaluating.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Check</th>
          <th>Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Pricing in ZAR</td><td>Avoids budgeting against a currency that moves against you month to month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Local support hours</td><td>Support that runs in South African working hours</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cloud hosting</td><td>Your team needs an internet connection. No server room or generator plan of your own required</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Data export and ownership</td><td>A written commitment that you can export documents, metadata and audit history if you leave</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Audit trail</td><td>A record of who approved what, and when, that your certification body can review directly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Operator role and DPA</td><td>A data processing agreement that states the vendor's role and obligations under POPIA</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    On <strong>cloud hosting and load-shedding</strong>, moving to a hosted system doesn't make
    load-shedding disappear. It moves the uptime problem from your own server room to your vendor's
    infrastructure and your team's internet connection. What it does remove is your dependency on a single
    office's power and a single physical server. Your team can reach a cloud-hosted system from anywhere
    with a connection, which a locally-installed system tied to one building cannot offer.
  </p>

