  <h2>A short checklist for evaluating ISO 9001 software in South Africa</h2>
  <p>
    Use this list as a script for a first call with any vendor. A vendor that can answer all nine plainly,
    in writing, is one worth shortlisting.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Where exactly are your servers, and who owns the infrastructure?</li>
    <li>Are you the responsible party or the operator for the data we put into your system?</li>
    <li>Is a POPIA data processing agreement available, and what does it say about cross-border transfer?</li>
    <li>Is pricing quoted in ZAR, and is it VAT-inclusive?</li>
    <li>What are your support hours, and where is your support team based?</li>
    <li>Can we export our full document set, metadata and audit trail if we leave, at no extra cost?</li>
    <li>Does your audit trail show who approved what, and when, in a format our certification body can review?</li>
    <li>Do you support single sign-on, multi-factor authentication or electronic signatures? If not, what is the alternative?</li>
    <li>Are you claiming ISO 9001 certification for your software? No software can hold one, so ask them to explain how.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    None of these questions are unreasonable to ask. A vendor built for South African SMEs should answer
    every one without escalating to a sales engineer. If a vendor hedges on hosting location, data export
    or what "ISO 9001 software" means, treat that as an answer in itself.
  </p>
